# ADR Format and Lifecycle

Store ADRs in `docs/adr/` and use numeric, kebab-case file names:

- `0001-short-title.md`
- `0002-next-decision.md`

## ADR Structure

Use the sections in `docs/adr/0000-template.md`, in that order.

## Lifecycle Rules

- `proposed`: the PR is open. Never merge an ADR as `proposed`; set `accepted` in
  the PR. Merging is the acceptance.
- `accepted`: decided and editable until the decision is implemented or released,
  even if the status has not been updated yet. Note each change in the ADR.
- `implemented`: implemented or released. It can only be superseded. For a
  decision built over several PRs, set this when the last one merges.
- `superseded` / `deprecated`: replaced or no longer applies. Link the replacement.
- ADRs accepted before this rule (0068 and lower, except 0053 and 0067) can only
  be superseded, whatever their status says. Their status line may still be
  updated.
- Keep one decision per ADR.

## Confirmation

Every new ADR lists how the decision is checked. Each item names a test or a bench
check.

## Traceability Rules

When implementation follows an ADR, reference the ADR in:

- commit message or PR description
- implementation notes
- related technical documentation
